    "seo": {
      "title": "Transaction Management Systems in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented payment-management capabilities and the evidence gaps around reconciliation, settlement, and operational performance."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "documented-capabilities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The work surrounding a payment",
        "content": "A useful concrete example comes from ZarinPal’s provider-published Node.js software development kit, a library intended to help applications communicate with its payment service. Its README lists separate methods for creating a payment request, obtaining a redirect URL, verifying a payment, and asking about a transaction. It also documents fee calculation, refund and reversal requests, and a list of unverified payments. [s1]\n\nThese distinctions show why transaction management extends beyond presenting a payment button. The published interface exposes several operations concerning the same commercial activity. The repository establishes that these capabilities are documented; it does not independently demonstrate production availability, successful processing, or suitability for every merchant. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "capability-map",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Documented payment-management capabilities",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"ZarinPal SDK API\"] -->|\"Payment initiation\"| B[\"Create request and obtain redirect URL\"]\n  A -->|\"Payment confirmation\"| C[\"Verify payment\"]\n  A -->|\"Transaction lookup\"| D[\"Inquire about transaction\"]\n  A -->|\"Adjustment requests\"| E[\"Refund or reverse payment\"]\n  A -->|\"Outstanding verification lookup\"| F[\"List unverified payments\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows group methods listed in the provider’s README. They do not represent a required processing sequence, guaranteed outcomes, or a national architecture.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment-questions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From an interface to an operational system",
        "content": "An API inventory is a starting point for investigation. A fuller assessment should ask how a product associates payments with merchant orders, handles repeated notifications, resolves uncertain outcomes, and compares its records with settlement evidence. These are editorial evaluation questions, not verified claims about the inspected provider.\n\nThe available repository does not establish a complete reconciliation workflow or the contractual meaning of each adjustment. This draft therefore makes no claim that verification proves merchant settlement, that a refund request guarantees repayment, or that one provider’s interface represents all Iranian transaction systems.",
