    "seo": {
      "title": "Financial Technology in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An evidence-led introduction to Iran’s fintech landscape, its distinct financial services, regulatory history, and unresolved research gaps."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-and-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Finance delivered through digital technology",
        "content": "Financial technology, usually shortened to fintech, means applying digital technology to financial services. The World Bank treats this transformation as a change affecting competition, financial infrastructure, and supervision as well as customer-facing products. Its analysis emphasizes that embedding finance in other services can blur the boundary of the financial sector. [s1]\n\nFor readers exploring Iran, this offers a useful organizing principle: examine the financial function behind the interface. The questions raised by a payment service differ from those raised by credit or investment. A convenient application alone does not establish the quality of the underlying financial arrangement.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-regulatory-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A landscape with several regulatory histories",
        "content": "The International Bar Association’s 2024 Iran chapter reported that the country lacked an overarching fintech law at that time. It described Central Bank of Iran measures concerning payments, wallets, and digital banking, alongside capital-market rules affecting crowdfunding and algorithmic trading. This dated account establishes a differentiated regulatory landscape; it cannot establish which provisions remained applicable at the research cutoff. [s2]\n\nThe chapter also reported no dedicated open-banking regulations in 2024. That statement must remain attached to its date. It should not be read as proof that bank interfaces were absent, or as a verified description of the framework in October 2026. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exploring-the-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Questions that separate the main branches",
        "content": "The pages in this branch organize research around payment facilitators and gateways, digital wallets, open banking, digital credit assessment, crowdfunding, digital investment, and asset management. Useful questions include who provides the underlying financial service, what information the customer authorizes others to access, and which institution carries responsibility when something goes wrong.\n\nFor a wallet, investigate how balances are held and redeemed. For credit assessment, investigate the evidence used and how errors can be challenged. For crowdfunding and investment services, investigate the investor’s actual claim and the disclosures supporting it. These are questions for evaluating evidence, not assertions that any particular Iranian product offers those protections.",
        "source_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "inclusion-and-outcomes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access is a starting point",
        "content": "The Committee on Payments and Market Infrastructures and the World Bank’s 2020 work on financial inclusion argues that fintech can improve access to transaction accounts and encourage their use, while stressing that accompanying risks require management. It places technology within a broader framework rather than treating innovation as sufficient evidence of inclusion. [s3]\n\nApplied as an editorial lens, this means asking whether people can use a service reliably and understand its costs and obligations. National averages would also need examination for differences between user groups and locations. No verified evidence inspected for this draft supports a claim about which Iranian regions or populations benefit most.\n\nIran’s fintech landscape is best explored service by service, with dated evidence about institutions, products, and outcomes. This draft supplies a framework and a limited regulatory baseline; further research is necessary before it can serve as a current sector guide.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
